{"name": "exemplar_attention_similarity", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "People do not infer a rule; they judge the target by its summed similarity to the stored examples (exemplar-based generalization, as in the Generalized Context Model). Each number is represented on a few psychological dimensions — magnitude, parity, final digit and tens digit — each with its own attention weight, similarity to an example decays exponentially with attention-weighted mismatch, and the probability of \"yes\" rises with the target's average similarity to the examples.", "context": "experiment1 round 0 candidate 0 lens 0"}
{"name": "non_accumulating_size_principle", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "People do Bayesian concept learning over the same rules and intervals, but they do not accumulate the size-principle evidence across examples: they judge how well a concept fits the examples by the average fit of one example, sharpened by a fixed amount, so their generalisation is no tighter after four examples than after one. This disagrees most with the current best model on sets with one or two examples, where it predicts sharper, more rule-like generalisation, and on four-example sets, where it predicts broader, more graded generalisation that keeps giving partial \"yes\" to targets that fit only a looser concept (for example, odd numbers that are not in a narrow rule the examples happen to share).", "context": "experiment1 round 0 candidate 1 lens 1"}
{"name": "exemplar_feature_similarity", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "People do not infer a rule at all; they judge a target by its summed similarity to the remembered examples (exemplar generalization), where similarity between two numbers falls off exponentially with how many simple arithmetic properties they disagree on (parity, divisibility by 3-10, last digit, tens digit, being prime, square or a power of two) plus how far apart they are in magnitude. A target that closely resembles even one example is accepted, with no size-principle penalty and no requirement that it fit all examples jointly.", "context": "experiment1 round 0 candidate 2 lens 2"}
{"name": "bayesian_concepts_with_guessing_lapse", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "A refinement of bigelow_piantadosi_independent: people still judge by Bayesian concept learning over the same 45 rules and all intervals of 1-100 (independently weighted rule prior, size principle), but on a fraction of trials they do not consult that posterior at all and instead give a guess, saying \"yes\" with their own fixed guessing rate. The one change is this response-lapse stage, which explains why even targets that are among the examples are often rejected and why clearly excluded targets are sometimes accepted.", "context": "experiment1 round 0 candidate 3 refine incumbent bigelow_piantadosi_independent"}
